  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "The least value of magnetic field strength required to magnetize is nickel because it is a ferromagnetic material. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ni</mi></math> is a ferromagnetic substance which gets strongly magnetized when placed in an external magnetic field. When external magnetic field <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>, is applied the magnetic domains orient themselves in the direction of magnetic filed and grow in size.<br /><br />Hence, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ni</mi></math>&nbsp;requires least value of magnetic strength. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaCl</mi></math> is a diamagnetic material. Its field lines are repelled.",
